s (opens in new tab)†status.arxiv.org】\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nMore Optimizations to Sum-Check Proving (https://eprint.iacr.org/2024/1210)\nciteturn1view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: yesterday; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://eprint.iacr.org/2024/1210\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 73\nL0: # What a lovely hat\nL1: #### Is it made out of 【0†tin foil†iacr.org】?\nL2: \nL3: 【1†Cryptology ePrint Archive】\nL4: \nL5:   * 【2†Papers】 Updates from the last:\nL6:     * 【3†7 days】\nL7:     * 【4†31 days】\nL8:     * 【5†6 months】\nL9:     * 【6†365 days】\nL10:     * * * *\nL11: \nL12:     * 【7†Listing by year】\nL13:     * 【8†All papers】\nL14:     * 【9†Compact view】\nL15:     * 【10†Subscribe†www.iacr.org】\nL16:     * * * *\nL17: \nL18:     * 【11†How to cite】\nL19:     * * * *\nL20:     * 【12†Harvesting metadata】\nL21:   * 【2†Submissions】\nL22:     * 【13†Submit a paper】\nL23:     * 【14†Revise or withdraw a paper】\nL24:     * 【15†Acceptance and publishing conditions】\nL25: \nL26: 【16†Image: Search Button】\nL27: \nL28: [Input] [Button: Search ]\nL29: #### Paper 2024/1210\nL30: \nL31: ### 【17†More Optimizations to Sum-Check Proving】\nL32: \nL33: Quang Dao, Carnegie Mellon University\nL34: \nL35: Justin Thaler, Georgetown University, a16z crypto research\nL36: ##### Abstract\nL37: Many fast SNARKs apply the sum-check protocol to an $n$-variate polynomial of the form $g(x) = \\text{eq}(w,x) \\cdot p(x)$, where $p$ is a product of multilinear polynomials, $w \\in \\mathbb{F}^n$ is a random vector, and $\\text{eq}$ is the multilinear extension of the equality function. In this setting, we describe an optimization to the sum-check prover that substantially reduces the cost coming from the $\\text{eq}(w, x)$ factor.\nL38: Our work further improves on a prior optimization by Gruen (ePrint 2023), and in the small-field case, can be combined with additional optimizations by Bagad, Domb, and Thaler (ePrint 2024), and Dao and Thaler (ePrint 2024). Over large prime-order fields, our optimization eliminates roughly $2^{n + 1}$ field multiplications compared to a standard linear-time implementation of the prover, and roughly $2^{n-1}$ field multiplications when considered on top of Gruen's optimization.\nL39: These savings are about a 25% (respectively 10%) end-to-end prover speedup in common use cases, and potentially even larger when working over binary tower fields.\nL40: ##### Metadata\nL41: \nL42: Available format(s)\nL43:     【17†PDF】\nL44: Category\nL45:     【18†Cryptographic protocols】\nL46: Publication info\nL47:     Preprint.\nL48: Keywords\nL49:     【19†sum-check protocol】【20†interactive proofs】【21†SNARKs】\nL50: Contact author(s)\nL51:     justin r thaler @ gmail com\nL52: History\nL53:     2024-07-29: approved\nL54:     2024-07-27: received\nL55:     【22†See all versions】\nL56: Short URL\nL57:     【23†https://ia.cr/2024/1210†ia.cr】\nL58: License\nL59: \nL60: 【24†CC BY†creativecommons.org】\nL61: BibTeX 【25†Image】Copy to clipboard\nL62: \nL63:     @misc{cryptoeprint:2024/1210,\nL64:           author = {Quang Dao and Justin Thaler},\nL65:           title = {More Optimizations to Sum-Check Proving},\nL66:           howpublished = {Cryptology {ePrint} Archive, Paper 2024/1210},\nL67:           year = {2024},\nL68:           url = {https://eprint.iacr.org/2024/1210}\nL69:     }\nL70: \nL71: \nL72: Note: In order to protect the privacy of readers, eprint.iacr.org does not use cookies or embedded third party content.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nGitHub - compsec-epfl/efficient-sumcheck · GitHub (https://github.com/compsec-epfl/efficient-sumcheck)\nciteturn1view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 days ago; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://github.com/compsec-epfl/efficient-sumcheck\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 390\nL0: 【0†Skip to content】\nL1: ## Navigation Menu\nL2: \nL3: 【1†Sign in】\nL4: \n