3†Security】\nL213: \nL214: More items\nL215: # Efficient Sumcheck\nL216: \nL217: A high-performance sumcheck library with 【94†correctness-fuzzing】 against a verified oracle.\nL218: \nL219:   * Efficient — transparent SIMD acceleration (8-wide AVX-512, 2-wide NEON)\nL220:   * Streaming-capable — optional sublinear memory via sequential evaluation\nL221:   * Complete — built-in Fiat-Shamir, partial execution, per-round hooks\nL222: \nL223: Built using 【95†arkworks】. Compatible with any ecosystem — see 【96†`docs/compatibility.md`】.\nL224: \nL225: ## Quick Start\nL226: ### Multilinear Sumcheck\nL227: \nL228: Proves $H = \\sum_{x \\in \\lbrace 0,1 \\rbrace^v} p(x)$ where $p$ is a multilinear polynomial.\nL229: \nL230:     use effsc::{noop_hook, runner::sumcheck};\nL231:     use effsc::provers::multilinear::MultilinearProver;\nL232: \nL233:     let mut prover = MultilinearProver::new(evals);\nL234:     let proof = sumcheck(\nL235:         &mut prover,\nL236:         num_vars,\nL237:         &mut transcript,\nL238:         noop_hook,\nL239:     );\nL240: ### Inner Product Sumcheck\nL241: \nL242: Proves $H = \\sum_{x \\in \\lbrace 0,1 \\rbrace^v} f(x) \\cdot g(x)$ for two multilinear polynomials. Degree-2 round polynomials.\nL243: \nL244:     use effsc::{noop_hook, runner::sumcheck};\nL245:     use effsc::provers::inner_product::InnerProductProver;\nL246: \nL247:     let mut prover = InnerProductProver::new(a, b);\nL248:     let proof = sumcheck(\nL249:         &mut prover,\nL250:         num_vars,\nL251:         &mut transcript,\nL252:         noop_hook,\nL253:     );\nL254: ### Coefficient Sumcheck\nL255: Proves $H = \\sum_{x \\in \\lbrace 0,1 \\rbrace^v} p(x)$ where $\\deg_{x_i}(p) \\leq d$. The user implements `RoundPolyEvaluator` to define per-pair round polynomial contributions; the library handles iteration, parallelism, and reductions.\nL256: \nL257:     use effsc::{noop_hook, runner::sumcheck};\nL258:     use effsc::provers::coefficient::CoefficientProver;\nL259: \nL260:     let mut prover = CoefficientProver::new(\nL261:         &evaluator,\nL262:         tablewise,\nL263:         pairwise,\nL264:     );\nL265:     let proof = sumcheck(\nL266:         &mut prover,\nL267:         num_rounds,\nL268:         &mut transcript,\nL269:         noop_hook,\nL270:     );\nL271: ### Verification\nL272: One verifier for any degree $d$. Returns `SumcheckResult { challenges, final_claim }` — ⚠️ the caller is responsible for the oracle check (【97†Thaler Remark 4.2†people.cs.georgetown.edu】).\nL273: \nL274:     use effsc::{noop_hook_verify, verifier::sumcheck_verify};\nL275: \nL276:     let result = sumcheck_verify(\nL277:         claimed_sum,\nL278:         degree,\nL279:         num_rounds,\nL280:         &mut transcript,\nL281:         noop_hook_verify,\nL282:     )?;\nL283: \nL284:     // Standalone: compare against the prover's claimed final value.\nL285:     assert_eq!(result.final_claim, proof.final_value);\nL286: \nL287:     // Composed (WHIR, GKR): pass final_claim to the next layer.\nL288:     next_layer_claim = result.final_claim;\nL289: ## Variable Ordering\nL290: \nL291: Each prover comes in two variants:\nL292: \nL293:   * MSB (half-split) — optimal memory layout in most cases. Used by WHIR and WARP.\nL294:   * LSB (pair-split) — optimal for streaming applications where evaluations arrive sequentially.\nL295: MSB  | LSB\nL296: --- | ---\nL297: `MultilinearProver`  | `MultilinearProverLSB`\nL298: `InnerProductProver`  | `InnerProductProverLSB`\nL299: `CoefficientProver`  | `CoefficientProverLSB`\nL300: `EqFactoredProver`  | —\nL301: `GkrProver`  | —\nL302: \nL303: See 【98†`docs/design.md`】 for details.\nL304: ## Partial Execution and Hooks\nL305: \nL306: The `sumcheck()` runner supports partial execution (`num_rounds < v`) and per-round hooks for composed protocols:\nL307: \nL308:     // WHIR: partial sumcheck with proof-of-work grinding\nL309:     let proof = sumcheck(\nL310:         &mut prover,\nL311:         folding_factor,  // num_rounds < v\nL312:         &mut transcript,\nL313:         |_, t| round_pow.prove(t),  // per-round hook\nL314:     );\nL315: ## SIMD Acceleration\nL316: \nL317: All provers transparently auto-dispatch to SIMD backends. Supported fields:\nL318: \nL319:   * [Input] Goldilocks ($p = 2^{64} - 2^{32} + 1$) and degree-2/3 extensions\nL320:   * [Input] M31 ($p = 2^{31} - 1$) and extensions\nL321:   * [Input] BabyBear ($p = 2^{31} - 2^{27} + 1$) and extensions\nL322:   * [Input] KoalaBear ($p = 2^{31} - 2^{24} + 1$) and extensions\nL323: Backend  | Width  | Platform\nL324: --- | --- | ---\nL325: NEON  | 2-wide  | aarch64 (Apple M-series, Graviton)\nL326: AVX-512 IFMA  | 8-wide  | x86_64 (Sapphire Rapids)\nL327: Scalar for other fields. See 【99†`SECURITY.md`】.\nL328: ## Examples\nL329: \nL330: Integrated into Whir (【100†PR】) and Warp (【101†PR】) with measured performance improvements. Integration capability for streaming contexts like 【102†Jolt】 is described in 【98†`docs/design.md`】.\nL331: \nL332: ## Correctness\nL333: \nL334: 🚧 Undergoing fuzzing over randomized inputs against 【103†z-tech/sumcheck-lean4】, an oracle with machine-checked proofs of completeness and soundness. Findings to follow.\nL335: ## References\nL336: \nL337: [【104†LFKN92†dl.acm.org】]: Carsten Lund, Lance Fortnow, Howard J. Karloff, and Noam Nisan. \"Algebraic Methods for Interactive Proof Systems\". In: Journal of the ACM 39.4 (1992).\nL338: \nL339: [【105†CTY11†arxiv.org】]: Graham Cormode, Justin Thaler, and Ke Yi. \"Verifying computations with streaming interactive proofs\". In: Proceedings of the VLDB Endowment 5.1 (2011), pp. 25-36.\nL340: [【106†VSBW13†ieeexplore.ieee.org】]: Victor Vu, Srinath Setty, Andrew J. Blumberg, and Michael Walfish. \"A hybrid architecture for interactive verifiable computation\". In: Proceedings of the 34th IEEE Symposium on Security and Privacy. Oakland '13. 2013, pp. 223-237.\nL341: \nL342: [【107†CFFZ24†eprint.iacr.org】]: Alessandro Chiesa, Elisabetta Fedele, Giacomo Fenzi, Andrew Zitek-Estrada. \"A time-space tradeoff for the sumcheck prover\". In: Cryptology ePrint Archive.\nL343: [【108†BCFFMMZ25†eprint.iacr.org】]: Anubhav Baweja, Alessandro Chiesa, Elisabetta Fedele, Giacomo Fenzi, Pratyush Mishra, Tushar Mopuri, and Andrew Zitek-Estrada. \"Time-Space Trade-Offs for Sumcheck\". In: TCC Theory of Cryptography: 23rd International Conference, pp. 37.\nL344: \nL345: [【97†Thaler23†people.cs.georgetown.edu】]: Justin Thaler. \"Proofs, Arguments, and Zero-Knowledge\". Chapter 4: Interactive Proofs. July 2023.\nL346: [【109†BDDT25†eprint.iacr.org】]: Aarushi Bagad, Quang Dao, Yuri Domb, and Justin Thaler. \"Speeding Up Sum-Check Proving\". Cryptology ePrint Archive, 2025/1117.\nL347: ## About\nL348: \nL349: No description, website, or topics provided.\nL350: \nL351: ### Resources\nL352: \nL353: 【71†Readme】\nL354: \nL355: Apache-2.0, MIT licenses found\nL356: \nL357: ### Security policy\nL358: \nL359: 【72†Security policy】\nL360: \nL361: 【73†Activity】\nL362: \nL363: 【74†Custom properties】\nL364: \nL365: ### Stars\nL366: \nL367: 55 stars\nL368: \nL369: ### Watchers\nL370: \nL371: 5 watching\nL372: \n"
